# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("How deep does the base go under a residential lawn?",
         "<p>Depth is only part of the story for a {city} lawn's subgrade: the rule allows two to four inches of washed, open-graded crushed rock or crushed concrete, and we build toward the top of that range in two separate compacted passes rather than dumping it all at once. A yard compacted in a single pass looks finished the same day, but the material several inches down never gets the same pressure the top inch does, and that difference is what turns into an uneven dip years later. Splitting the layer in two, with a pass over each before adding the next, is a small change in sequence that changes how a {city} lawn holds up under a decade of foot traffic.</p>"),
        ("Why the subgrade slopes away from the house",
         "<p>Grade comes before rock on a {city} lawn: the bare soil gets shaped to fall one to two percent moving away from the foundation, following the direction water was already heading before construction started, whether that's toward a rear fence line, a side yard low point, or a street-facing curb. That fall gets rechecked with a level once the first lift of rock is compacted, since two to four inches of stone can flatten a subtle slope if nobody's watching for it. A lawn shaped this way sends a downpour toward the yard's natural low spot instead of pooling against the house, which is the entire point of doing this step in {city} before turf ever gets involved.</p>"),
        ("What washed crushed rock does that unwashed fill can't",
         "<p>The state's turf rule specifically requires the crushed rock or crushed concrete under a lawn to be washed before it goes in, and there's a reason beyond paperwork. Unwashed material carries fine dust and clay particles that settle into the gaps between the larger stones once they get wet, and over a season or two those fines bind together into a hard, almost solid crust. A crust like that stops water from passing through the base at all, turning a yard that should drain in minutes into one that holds a puddle for days. Washed rock keeps those gaps open on a {city} lot long after installation, which is what lets the layer underneath keep doing its job.</p>"),
        ("Building above a sandy water table in {city}",
         "<p>{city} sits on native soil that's mostly fine sand, and after a heavy wet season that sand can stay saturated within a foot of grade far longer than most homeowners expect, sometimes well into the following month. A base built at the shallow end of the state's two-to-four-inch range barely lifts the turf above that saturation zone, so we lean toward the fuller depth on any lot where drainage already looks marginal before construction starts. The extra couple of inches doesn't change how the base is built, only how much separation exists between the turf and ground that's still holding last month's rain.</p>"),
        ("Why the base still has to drain faster than the storm",
         "<p>Perforated turf backing on its own passes water at more than thirty inches an hour, but that number means little if the base underneath can't move water at a similar rate. A Central Florida cell can drop one to three inches of rain in under an hour during a typical {city} summer, and a base compacted too tight, or built from unwashed fill that's crusted over, can't keep up with that pace even though the turf on top clearly can. When a new lawn puddles after a storm, the base is almost always the reason, not a defect in the turf itself.</p>"),
        ("What changes on a corner lot's two frontages",
         "<p>A corner lot in {city} usually faces a street or right-of-way on two sides instead of one, and the base plan has to answer to both rather than pitching everything toward a single rear low point the way an interior lot does. Compacted rock still runs two to four inches deep, but the crew often splits the one to two percent grade toward two separate exit points, one along each frontage, so water leaving the property has somewhere legal to go on either side. Skipping that split and grading toward just one corner can send more runoff toward a neighbor's frontage than the state's rule against added runoff actually allows.</p>"),
        ("Keeping the septic pump-out lid inside the base plan",
         "<p>Any {city} lot on a septic system needs its tank's pump-out lid mapped before rock goes down, since the state's turf rule requires that access stay reachable once the lawn is finished, not buried under compacted fill and turf like the rest of the yard. The crew builds the base up to the lid's edge rather than over it, and the finished grade around that spot gets checked separately so a pump-out truck years later isn't chasing a lid buried under an inch of settled infill and blades. This step happens during grading, before compaction locks the surrounding base in place.</p>"),
        ("Feathering the base where it meets a driveway or patio",
         "<p>Wherever a {city} lawn's new base runs up against an existing driveway, patio, or walkway, the compacted rock has to taper down to match that slab's height rather than stopping abruptly at its edge. A base left too high creates a ridge the turf then has to bridge, and one left too low leaves a gap that collects standing water right at the transition. Getting that feathered height right happens during the grading and compaction stage, before turf ever gets rolled out, since correcting it afterward means pulling material back up rather than just adjusting a seam.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Choosing a backing that actually drains",
         "<p>Every residential turf product we install uses a perforated backing built to pass water at well over thirty inches an hour, since a backing that seals up under a Central Florida storm defeats the point of a base built to drain. Two backing styles come up most on a {city} lawn: a lighter perforated polyurethane for a budget-first job, and a heavier, fully permeable, double-stitched version for a full-sun front lawn under years of foot traffic. Both meet the same drainage requirement; the difference is how much infill weight and stitching the backing can carry over a decade of afternoon rain before it starts to sag or stretch at the seams.</p>"),
        ("What infill goes into a plain lawn",
         "<p>A residential lawn takes silica sand as infill in most cases, brushed in at roughly one to two pounds per square foot to weigh the backing down and keep the blades standing upright instead of matting flat. Coated silica sand and zeolite are also allowed under the state's natural-material rule, and either one can go into a plain lawn if a {city} homeowner wants the odor control those products were built for, even without a pet in the yard. What never goes on a residential lawn is rubber or another synthetic infill, since the rule reserves that material for the ground directly under playground equipment, not for grass a family walks across barefoot.</p>"),
        ("How product choice affects a ten- to twenty-year lawn",
         "<p>Lifespan for a residential lawn runs ten to twenty years, and where a given yard lands in that range depends on UV stabilization in the fiber, how much traffic the lawn takes, and which infill was chosen at installation. A lighter economy-grade product on a {city} rental home, mowed hard by foot traffic and never topped off with infill, tends to mat and fade toward the shorter end of that window. A denser, better-stabilized blend with infill maintained over the years holds its color and stands back up after traffic longer, which is the main reason two lawns installed the same year can look years apart by their tenth summer.</p>"),
        ("What the residential price range buys in {city}",
         "<p>Installed synthetic lawn runs eight to eighteen dollars a square foot, with most yards landing between ten and sixteen. That range covers more than the turf roll itself: base material, compaction labor, seam tape and adhesive, and edge anchoring all sit inside the same number, and turf is typically under half of the total cost on a {city} job. Paying toward the lower end of the range usually means a lighter product on a straightforward, flat, easy-access yard, while the upper end reflects a heavier face weight, tighter stitching, or a lot with tricky access that slows the crew down regardless of which product goes on top.</p>"),
        ("Why crumb rubber stays off a residential lawn",
         "<p>Homeowners sometimes ask about rubber infill for a lawn since it comes up in general searches about synthetic turf, but Florida's rule draws a clear line: rubber and other synthetic infill products are permitted only inside the footprint of playground equipment, never across an open residential lawn in {city} where a family walks barefoot or a toddler sits down in the grass. A lawn's infill options are limited instead to silica sand, rock, shell, or a coated sand with a non-toxic finish. That distinction means the material under a backyard swing set and the material covering the rest of the same yard are never interchangeable, even though both sit a few feet apart on the same property.</p>"),
        ("What 'installed to the manufacturer's specifications' actually covers",
         "<p>Florida's turf rule requires an installation to follow the manufacturer's specifications, which in practice means the seam adhesive, nail gauge, and infill rate all have to match whatever that specific turf product's spec sheet calls for rather than a generic, one-size approach. A {city} homeowner switching products mid-quote, say from one backing weight to a heavier one, is also switching the spec sheet the crew has to follow for everything from seam overlap to infill weight. Two products installed to their own correct specifications can still look different once finished, since the numbers behind each build aren't identical.</p>"),
        ("Why infill grain size matters for staying on the property",
         "<p>The state's rule that infill must not wash off a lawn puts some weight on grain size, not just infill type, when choosing a product for a {city} yard with any real slope to it. A finer, lighter sand can lift and travel toward a low corner during a hard rain faster than a coarser grade of the same silica sand would, even though both meet the natural-material requirement. On a lawn graded toward a swale or a shared boundary, choosing a slightly heavier grain, and keeping the grade itself gentle, does more to keep infill in place than switching infill types altogether.</p>"),
        ("Reading the product line on a residential quote",
         "<p>A clear {city} residential quote spells out backing weight class, infill type and rate, and fiber material as separate line items rather than folding everything into one lump turf charge, since those three specs are what actually differ between a budget bid and a heavier-duty one for the same square footage. A quote missing infill rate, silica at roughly one to two pounds per square foot, for instance, leaves a homeowner unable to compare it against another bid apples to apples. Asking a contractor to itemize those three specs before signing is a fast way to see what a price difference is actually paying for.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("The order a lawn conversion actually happens in",
         "<p>A lawn conversion in {city} follows the same order every time regardless of yard size: strip the old sod and several inches of soil, shape the bare ground to grade, build and compact the rock base in lifts, roll out and seam the turf, then anchor the perimeter and brush in infill last. Skipping ahead, laying turf before the base has fully settled under compaction, or infilling before seams are trimmed tight, is where most callbacks trace back to later. Doing the steps in sequence, and not rushing past one to start the next, is most of what separates a lawn that holds up from one that doesn't.</p>"),
        ("Capping the sprinklers before turf goes down",
         "<p>Any spray head that used to water a section of {city} yard gets capped at the valve box before turf goes anywhere near that spot, since the state's rule bars using an in-ground irrigation system to water synthetic turf once it's installed. That step happens early in the process, usually right after strip-out and before the base goes in, so a crew isn't cutting into finished rock work later to reach a buried line. From that point on, a hose bib does the rinsing a lawn needs, and the old zone valve for that section typically just gets left capped and unused rather than removed.</p>"),
        ("Taping seams and anchoring edges on a {city} lawn",
         "<p>A {city} lawn's seams never rely on nails alone: rolls get cut precisely between stitch rows so the pattern lines up, then bonded from underneath with tape and adhesive before anyone walks across the join. The border around the whole lawn is handled separately, either galvanized fasteners spaced a few inches apart along open turf, or a paver and bender-board combination where the grass meets a garden bed. Both choices exist to satisfy the same standard: an edge or a seam has to survive a tropical-storm-strength wind and the standing water that follows it, not just look tidy on a calm afternoon.</p>"),
        ("Should a weed barrier go under a residential lawn?",
         "<p>A residential lawn in {city} can go either way on a weed fabric layer between the compacted rock and the turf backing, and it's an optional add rather than something the build requires. Most owners choose to include it as a long-term guard against stray growth pushing up through a seam years down the road, and it makes no difference to how quickly the base itself drains. Dog runs are the one exception: fabric under a pet area traps liquid at the surface rather than letting it filter down into the rock, which is why that layer gets skipped entirely for pet turf even though it's standard on a plain family lawn.</p>"),
        ("Scheduling a lawn install around a {city} rainy season",
         "<p>Rainy season in Central Florida runs June through September, with afternoon storms arriving on a near-daily schedule by midsummer, and that pattern shapes when a {city} lawn conversion gets scheduled more than most homeowners expect. Base compaction needs the ground reasonably dry to pack correctly, so a crew working through a wet stretch may pause a job partway through rather than compact rock into standing water. Dry-season installs, roughly October through May, tend to move start to finish without a weather-driven gap, which is part of why booking calendars fill up fastest in the cooler months even though the work itself is identical either time of year.</p>"),
        ("What to walk before the crew's truck leaves",
         "<p>Before a {city} crew packs up on install day, walking the finished lawn is worth the ten minutes it takes: run a hose across the surface and watch where water moves, check that seams sit flat underfoot without a ridge, and confirm infill looks even rather than piled in one corner. Catching a low spot or a thin infill patch on day one, while the crew can still address it, is far simpler than noticing the same issue after the first real storm. This walkthrough is separate from any paperwork and takes only a few minutes alongside the crew before they go.</p>"),
        ("Staking the ten-foot line before ordering turf for a pond lot",
         "<p>On a {city} lot backing onto a pond or canal, the crew stakes the required ten-foot setback line during layout, before ordering turf or base material, so the job is priced and measured against the actual buildable area rather than the full lot up to the water's edge. Skipping that step and ordering by rough lot dimensions risks ordering material for square footage that the setback rule doesn't allow to be turfed at all. Staking first also gives the homeowner a chance to see exactly where the usable lawn ends before any rock goes down.</p>"),
        ("Trimming turf at a driveway or patio edge",
         "<p>Once the base is feathered to meet an existing driveway or patio on a {city} lot, turf gets rolled across that boundary and trimmed tight to the hardscape edge as one of the last steps, after seams are set but before final infill goes in. That edge typically takes an adhesive-set bond rather than nails, since there's no soil at that specific point to anchor into, unlike the rest of the lawn's perimeter. Getting the trim line clean here matters more than elsewhere, since a driveway edge is one of the most visible and heavily walked transitions on the whole property.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("What summer sun does to an unshaded {city} lawn",
         "<p>Full Florida sun pushes a synthetic lawn's surface to 120 to 150°F, occasionally past 160°F, while natural grass nearby stays close to air temperature. That heat is mostly a comfort and safety issue for bare feet and pets rather than a defect in the product, but it catches homeowners off guard on a west-facing {city} front yard with no afternoon shade at all. A quick hose rinse drops that surface temperature 30 to 50 degrees within minutes, which is the fastest fix available, though a lighter-colored or cooling infill also helps more than most people expect once it's actually compared side by side.</p>"),
        ("The window that can melt a lawn from across the yard",
         "<p>Low-emissivity glass, common on newer replacement windows, reflects enough concentrated sunlight to soften a synthetic lawn from several feet away, since polyethylene blades start to give around 175 to 200°F under that kind of focused heat. The damage shows up as a scorched or melted patch in an odd shape that doesn't match any known cause until someone traces the sun's path back to a south- or west-facing window. It's worth checking any low-E glass near a {city} lawn before or after installation, since the fix, usually a window film or a repositioned pane of turf, is far cheaper than replacing a burned section later.</p>"),
        ("Staying outside a live oak's drip line in {city}",
         "<p>A live oak's root system runs well past the edge of its canopy, and the state's turf rule bars installing synthetic grass inside that drip line, on either the property it sits on or an adjacent one, unless a certified arborist certifies that the install won't cause harm. Skipping that step on a {city} lot with mature oaks risks damaging surface roots during base excavation, which can stress or eventually kill a tree that took decades to grow. Getting the arborist's letter first, before any digging starts near a canopy, is the only way around the setback rather than a shortcut past it.</p>"),
        ("Why a lawn can't cross a swale or hug a pond",
         "<p>Two setbacks apply to any {city} lawn near water: turf has to stay at least ten feet from a natural or man-made waterbody unless a seawall or bulkhead already separates the two, and it can never go inside a drainage swale, ditch, or a stormwater pond's littoral zone, no matter how flat and convenient that strip of ground looks. Both rules exist because turf and the base under it change how water moves across a lot, and a swale or pond edge is specifically where the state doesn't want that changed. A yard backing onto a retention pond usually has more usable space than these setbacks first suggest, just not right up to the water's edge.</p>"),
        ("What a rushed base looks like after one rainy season",
         "<p>A poorly built {city} lawn doesn't announce itself on installation day; the surface looks the same whether the crew took shortcuts or not. The first clue usually arrives with the season's heaviest rain: a corner that was stapled down instead of properly bonded starts to peel back, a section where compaction was skipped settles just enough to hold a puddle overnight, and unwashed fill that should never have gone under turf clogs with fines until water simply stops passing through it. None of that shows up in a same-day walkthrough, which is exactly why judging a lawn by its depth and material specifications matters more than judging it by how flat and green it looks the day the crew packs up.</p>"),
        ("When infill migrates off the property instead of staying put",
         "<p>Florida's turf rule requires that infill not wash off the property, and a {city} lawn graded steeply toward one low corner, or missing a proper edge at that corner, can shed a visible amount of silica sand or zeolite into a swale or a neighbor's yard during a hard summer storm. The fix isn't a different infill type; it's a gentler grade and a properly anchored edge at whatever point water actually exits the lot. A lawn losing infill at one corner every storm season usually needs a look at drainage direction, not a heavier brushing schedule.</p>"),
        ("Why a driveway edge is often the first to lift",
         "<p>The seam where a {city} lawn meets a driveway or patio takes more foot and wheel traffic than almost any other point on the property, and because that edge bonds to concrete with adhesive rather than anchoring into soil with nails, a skipped or rushed bond there tends to show trouble before the rest of the perimeter does. A lifted corner right at that transition after a season of use usually traces back to that specific bond rather than a problem with the lawn generally. Checking that one edge periodically catches most of what would otherwise become a larger repair.</p>"),
        ("The swale a corner lot doesn't expect on its side street",
         "<p>A corner lot in {city} often carries a drainage swale along both street frontages rather than just the one facing the front door, and the state's rule against installing turf inside a swale applies to that second, less obvious strip exactly as it does to the main one. Homeowners planning a full-yard conversion sometimes measure only from the front property line and miss how far the side-street swale actually reaches into the lot. Confirming both swale boundaries before finalizing a layout avoids having to pull installed turf back from a strip that should never have been included in the first place.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("Rinsing a lawn instead of watering it",
         "<p>A synthetic lawn never gets watered through an in-ground system, since the state's rule bars using irrigation heads on turf and any that used to serve the area get capped at installation. A hose rinse takes over that job instead, settling infill, clearing pollen, and cooling the surface 30 to 50 degrees on a hot {city} afternoon within minutes of turning the water on. Rinsing more often in peak summer than in a mild spring is normal, and there's no schedule to keep beyond doing it when the lawn looks dusty, smells faintly of pollen, or simply feels too hot to walk across barefoot.</p>"),
        ("Brushing against the grain to stop matting",
         "<p>Blades on a {city} lawn lie down naturally in high-traffic paths, a straight line to a trampoline, a gate, or a mailbox, and brushing against the grain a few times a year is what stands them back up before matting sets in permanently. A stiff push broom or a rented power brush both work, and the goal is lifting the fiber and redistributing infill evenly, not scrubbing hard enough to pull blades loose from the backing. Skipping this for several years in a row is usually what turns a worn path into a permanently flattened one that brushing alone can no longer fix.</p>"),
        ("Walking the perimeter after a {city} storm",
         "<p>After a tropical storm or a stretch of unusually hard rain, walking the full perimeter of a lawn is worth the ten minutes it takes, checking specifically for a corner or edge that's lifted where it was nailed or bordered. Wind and standing water are exactly what the state's anchoring rule is meant to withstand, so a lifted edge after a storm usually means that spot needs attention, not that the whole install failed. Catching it early on a {city} property, while it's still a small section, keeps the fix to a quick re-anchor instead of a larger repair once more water or foot traffic works the gap wider.</p>"),
        ("Topping off infill as a lawn ages",
         "<p>Infill goes in at roughly one to two pounds per square foot at installation, and that volume gradually drops over years of rinsing, rain, and UV exposure even on a lawn that's otherwise holding up well. A thin infill layer is one of the more common reasons an older {city} lawn starts to look flatter or feel firmer underfoot than it did when new, since the backing loses some of the weight that kept blades standing upright. Topping it off periodically, rather than waiting until the lawn looks visibly bare in spots, keeps a ten- to twenty-year lifespan lawn performing closer to the higher end of that range.</p>"),
        ("Clearing leaves before they reach the infill",
         "<p>Leaves and organic debris left sitting on a lawn break down into the infill layer over time, which changes drainage and can eventually feed odor or discoloration the same way it would on a natural yard. Blowing or raking a {city} lawn clear before leaves decompose matters most during a live oak's late-winter drop, when a single mature tree can shed enough material to blanket a small yard within a couple of weeks. This is a five-minute habit compared with the deeper clean needed once debris has already worked its way down into the backing and started to compost in place.</p>"),
        ("The hose test to run on installation day",
         "<p>Right after a {city} crew finishes, running a hose across the new lawn once is worth doing before they leave the property, watching for any spot where water sits rather than moving toward the graded low point. It's also the moment to note where a septic tank's pump-out lid sits relative to the new turf, since the state's rule requires that access stay reachable and it's easier to remember its exact spot on day one than to go searching for it under grown-in grass two years later. Both checks take only a few minutes and cost nothing beyond the water itself.</p>"),
        ("What a lawn's second year actually tells you",
         "<p>By a {city} lawn's second year, it's been through two full rainy seasons, which is enough time for any marginal drainage or a loosely anchored edge to have already shown itself at least once. A second-year check means walking the perimeter for anything that lifted and was never caught, confirming infill still sits close to its original one to two pounds per square foot, and checking that no nearby construction or new landscaping has changed the grade the lawn was originally built to shed water toward. A lawn that's cleared two rainy seasons without incident tends to keep performing the same way afterward.</p>"),
        ("Why rinsing habits shift between the wet and dry months",
         "<p>Care on a {city} lawn looks different in January than it does in July: dry-season months call for more frequent hose rinsing purely to manage heat and dust, since there's no regular rain doing any of that work. Once June through September rainy season arrives, storms handle a lot of the rinsing on their own, and the more useful habit becomes checking drainage and edges after each significant downpour rather than keeping to a fixed rinse schedule. Treating both seasons the same, either over-rinsing in the wet months or under-checking drainage in the dry ones, misses what each season actually needs.</p>"),
    ],
}
